{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass on the day that Moses finished setting up the Tabernacle</b> — It is not written <b>on the day he set up</b>, which teaches that during all seven days of the milu'im (inauguration) he would set it up and take it down, and on that day he set it up and did not take it down. Therefore it is written \"on the day that Moses finished setting up\" — on that day its erections were completed. And it was the first of the month of Nisan: on the second day the cow was burned, on the third day the first sprinkling was performed, on the seventh day they shaved — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 7:1), from the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory (Sifrei Naso 145). But this is not a conclusive proof, for \"on the day of finishing\" is not attached to \"setting up\" alone, but rather: on the day that Moses finished setting up the Tabernacle and anointing it and consecrating it and the altar and all its vessels, the princes offered their offering — when all this was done. And in any case it was on the eighth day. Now this passage was written here because on the first day of the milu'im He called to Moses from the Tent of Meeting, and HaShem spoke to him all the passages from the beginning of the book of Leviticus until \"And it came to pass on the eighth day\" (Leviticus 9:1), which deal with the laws of the sacrifices, and from the eighth day onward He spoke to him all the passages from the beginning of \"This is the living creature that you may eat\" (Leviticus 11:2), which deal with the laws of prohibition and permissibility, and all of them involve the subject of sacrifices, and these matters continued in sequence until this point, as I have explained (at the beginning of the book and above, Leviticus 5:6). And when He completed the commandments that Moses was commanded to speak to Israel — all of them being laws of the service and the sacrifices and the charge of the Tent of Meeting and its service — He then returned here to relate the freewill offering of the princes in their sacrifices, which were from the eighth day until the nineteenth of the month, or until the twelfth of the month, according to the words of our Rabbis (Seder Olam, chapter 7):" "And the sense of <b>and he anointed them and consecrated them</b> — Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Numbers 7:1) said that he anointed them with the anointing oil and consecrated them with the blood of the sin-offering, as it says there: \"And he cleansed the altar, and the blood he poured at the base of the altar, and consecrated it, to make atonement upon it\" (Leviticus 8:15). But this is not so, for thus it is said of the Tabernacle: \"And he anointed it and consecrated it and all its vessels\" (Numbers 7:1), and the Tabernacle was not consecrated with blood at all. Rather, \"and he anointed them to consecrate them\" — the anointing itself was the consecration." "And the sense of <b>and the altar and all its vessels</b> is connected to the beginning of the verse: on the day that Moses finished setting up the Tabernacle and all its vessels, and anointed them and consecrated them."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The final date phrase offers two alternatives (עד יום תשעה עשר / עד שנים עשר); translated literally as presented.", 1 "The final clause reads וימשחם לקדש אותם without an explicit subject or verb distinction; rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:23.122541618Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}